This directory contains a compatibility copy of the HTTP v2 runtime from:

  https://github.com/tursodatabase/libsql-client-go
  v0.0.0-20251219100830-236aa1ff8acc

The original MIT license and copyright notice are preserved in LICENSE.
UPSTREAM.json records the original paths and SHA-256 hashes for all 13 copied
runtime files. The public sqliteparser and sqliteparserutils packages remain
dependencies of the same pinned upstream module; they are not copied here.

The copied files retain their original protocol, SQL parsing, transaction,
response parsing and error text. Non-200 HTTP errors additionally preserve their
actual response status as private typed metadata, including error-body fallbacks
and body-read failures. This is not evidence that a write had no effect; the
engine uses it together with the operation stage to avoid uncertain write replay.
An unambiguous single execute error also retains a primary SQLITE_BUSY code as
private typed rejection evidence. Busy text, non-200 HTTP failures and batch or
mixed results cannot create that evidence. Error text and SQL remain unchanged.
Other local changes are private import paths and one cleanup manager shared by
each opened database handle (one SQL pool).
Each accepted detached
stream-close request has a five-second deadline established before its goroutine
starts. At most 16 cleanup HTTP requests run per opener; capacity overflow is skipped without
queueing or waiting and increments a skip counter. A completed cleanup worker
reports skipped work with one fixed safe warning after releasing HTTP resources
and admission capacity; the foreground Close path never invokes the logger.
Skipped or failed cleanup relies on server expiry rather than confirmed release.
Cleanup remains asynchronous, does not retry, and is not synchronously joined by
database/sql Close. Serial repeated Close does not resubmit the captured baton.
The five-second deadline bounds the network request, not an arbitrary application
logger. At most one completed worker can remain in a blocked warning callback;
it holds no HTTP resource or cleanup slot and cannot delay foreground SQL or Close.

conn.go adapts the upstream legacy Driver.Open HTTP entry point and preserves
its auth-token aliases, query rejection, TLS normalization, and Host handling.
WebSocket and file connections continue to use the original upstream driver.

This is a bounded compatibility patch, not a replacement protocol or a general
driver fork. Review any future delta against the recorded upstream source;
do not silently change commit uncertainty, logical-stream pooling, or SQL types.
